<li about="#cite_note-military_objective-16" id="cite_note-military_objective-16" data-mw-footnote-number="16"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwAZ0"><a href="#cite_ref-military_objective_16-0" id="mwAZ4"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAZ8">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-military_objective_16-1" id="mwAaA"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAaE">2</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-military_objective-16" class="mw-reference-text reference-text">Article 52 of <i id="mwAaI">Additional <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protocol_I" title="Protocol I" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAaM">Protocol I</a> to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geneva_Conventions" title="Geneva Conventions" id="mwAaQ">Geneva Conventions</a> provides a widely accepted definition of military objective: "In so far as objects are concerned, military objectives are limited to those objects which by their nature, location, purpose or use make an effective contribution to military action and whose total or partial destruction, capture or neutralization, in the circumstances ruling at the time, offers a definite military advantage", <span about="#mwt70" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAaU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"harv","href":"./Template:Harv"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Moreno-Ocampo"},"2":{"wt":"2006"},"p":{"wt":"5, footnote 11"}},"i":0}}]}'>(</span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#CITEREFMoreno-Ocampo2006" about="#mwt70" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwAaY">Moreno-Ocampo 2006</a><span about="#mwt70" id="mwAac">, p.</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt70" id="mwAag"> </span><span about="#mwt70" id="mwAak">5, footnote 11)</span></i>.</span></li>
